+**A. Replay payload hash and conflict contract (DECIDED 2026-11-06; specification only, not implemented).** *Replaces the first-pass text of A (consolidated pass, C1). The first-pass rules are restated below, with the field lists, normalisation and encoding made exact.*
+1. *Domain and version.* The first hashed field is the encoding and domain version, the text `qrquick-licence-request-v1`. It is part of the hashed bytes. Any change to the field list, field order, type tags, normalisation or encoding is a deliberate version change (item 9).
+2. *Common fields, in this fixed order.* Every common field is encoded in every request. A field that is not supplied is encoded as an explicit null.
+   1. Encoding and domain version (text).
+   2. Caller UID (uuid), taken from the verified JWT subject (`auth.uid()`), never from a request parameter.
+   3. Shop ID (uuid).
+   4. Action (text): `grant`, `extend`, `revoke` or `replace`.
+   5. Expected state version (integer).
+   6. Confirmation value (boolean), `confirm`.
+   7. Reason (text), after the normalisation in item 5 and rule S.
+3. *Action-specific fields, in this fixed order, after the common fields.* The list for each action is fixed. A field that is not in the list for its action is rejected under item 4.
+   - *Grant:* (a) grant kind (text): `one_month`, `one_year`, `custom` or `lifetime`; (b) start input (timestamp, date-only or null); (c) end input (timestamp, date-only or null); (d) backdate confirmation (boolean). The 1-month and 1-year kinds fix their own duration, so Grant has no separate duration input.
+   - *Extend:* (a) extension unit (text): `month` or `year`. Extend takes no start, end or backdate input, because its start is calculated by the server (§12A.6, O3). Any such field is rejected.
+   - *Revoke:* (a) target coverage ID (uuid).
+   - *Replace:* (a) target coverage ID (uuid); (b) replacement grant kind (as Grant (a)); (c) replacement start input; (d) replacement end input; (e) replacement backdate confirmation. This set mirrors the Grant inputs. Which of them each action accepts is fixed by §12A.6 and implementation review. A replacement input that the action does not accept is an unknown field, so the hash still covers every input the RPC reads.
+4. *Unknown fields.* Any field outside the list for the request's action is rejected before hashing, with a specific committed validation code. It is not ignored. It writes no replay-ledger row and no denial row. A client-supplied hash, UID or other derived value is an unknown field. `request_id` is the lookup key and is not hashed.
+5. *Normalisation before hashing.*
+   - Timestamps: converted to UTC and encoded as a count of microseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z. An input with more than six fractional digits is rejected.
+   - Date-only inputs (`YYYY-MM-DD`): converted to 00:00 Asia/Kolkata on that date, then encoded as a timestamp. A date-only input and a timestamp that name the same instant produce the same hash.
+   - Text: normalised to Unicode NFC, then leading and trailing spaces (U+0020 only) are removed, as `btrim` does in 0011. The normalised value is the one hashed and stored. Enumerated text (action, grant kind, extension unit) must match an allowed token exactly after NFC, or it is rejected.
+   - UUIDs: encoded as their 16 bytes. The text form is not hashed.
+   - Integers: the expected state version is a non-negative decimal integer, with no sign and no leading zeros.
+   - Booleans: `true` or `false` only.
+   - Null: an explicit null, distinct from every value, including an empty string.
+6. *Canonical encoding.* The hash input is the concatenation of the encoded fields, in the order fixed by items 1 to 3. Each field is one type-tag byte, then a four-byte unsigned big-endian length of the value bytes, then the value bytes. The type tags are: `0x00` null (length 0, no value bytes); `0x01` boolean (length 1; `0x00` for false, `0x01` for true); `0x02` integer (ASCII decimal digits); `0x03` text (UTF-8, after item 5); `0x04` uuid (16 bytes); `0x05` timestamp (signed 64-bit big-endian count of microseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z). The digest is SHA-256 over the complete concatenation: 32 bytes, stored in `licence_requests.payload_hash` (0011 checks that the length is 32).
