+   - Different hash: conflict. Return a specific committed code (§12A.8 (ii)). Write nothing. It is not an authorisation denial and writes no denial row. The same `request_id` used with another shop gives a different hash, so it is a conflict.
+   - A conflicting ledger insert whose row is not visible on the required re-read: fail safely with an error. Write nothing. Never continue as a new operation.
+8. *Who computes it.* The server computes the hash from the normalised fields and the authenticated caller UID. A client never supplies a trusted hash.
+9. *Version change.* Changing the canonical format requires a deliberate version change: a new domain string (for example `qrquick-licence-request-v2`), a new encoder, new byte-exact test vectors, and a recorded decision on how existing ledger rows are compared. A v1 hash is never recomputed silently with another version's encoder. A stored hash-version column would be a schema change and is not approved (N.8).
